(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_biotechnologyprinciplesandprocesses=[{id:"neet-2026-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction endonucleases",q:`Which of the following statements are not true regarding restriction endonucleases?
A. They are called molecular scissors.
B. These are the enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophages in E. coli.
C. They cut the DNA only at the centre of the palindromic sites.
D. They remove nucleotides only from the ends of DNA fragments.
E. They recognise specific palindromic base-pair sequences.`,o:["A and B only","D and E only","C and D only","A and E only"],c:2,s:`Statements C and D are incorrect.
Restriction endonucleases usually cut the DNA slightly away from the centre of the palindrome sites.
They cannot remove nucleotides from the ends of a DNA fragment; that is the job of restriction exonuclease.`},{id:"neet-2026-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Tools of rDNA technology",q:`Match List I with List II:
List-I \u2014 List-II
A. Genetically modified organism \u2014 I. Agrobacterium tumefaciens
B. Thermostable DNA polymerase \u2014 II. Bt cotton
C. Ti plasmid \u2014 III. Thermus aquaticus
D. pBR322 \u2014 IV. Escherichia coli`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],c:2,s:`Genetically modified organism: Bt cotton.
Thermostable DNA polymerase: Thermus aquaticus.
Ti plasmid: Agrobacterium tumefaciens.
pBR322: Escherichia coli.`},{id:"neet-2026-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Polymerase chain reaction",q:"Identify the correct sequence of steps in each cycle of Polymerase Chain Reaction:",o:["Denaturation \u2192 Annealing \u2192 Extension","Denaturation \u2192 Extension \u2192 Annealing","Extension \u2192 Annealing \u2192 Denaturation","Annealing \u2192 Denaturation \u2192 Extension"],c:0,s:"The correct sequence of steps in PCR is Denaturation \u2192 Annealing \u2192 Extension."},{id:"neet-2026-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:`Which of the following statements are correct with respect to DNA separation, isolation and visualization?
A. The cutting of DNA is done by molecular scissors.
B. The DNA fragments separate according to their size in an agarose gel, upon electrophoresis.
C. The separated DNA fragments can be seen without staining when exposed to UV light.
D. The separated DNA fragments, when stained with ethidium bromide, can be seen in visible light.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A and D only","B and D only","B and C only","A and B only"],c:3,s:`DNA is cut by restriction enzymes (molecular scissors), giving DNA fragments.
The fragments are separated by gel electrophoresis.
They separate according to size through the sieving effect of the agarose gel.
They can be seen only after staining with ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation.
So A and B are correct; C and D are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2026-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Somatic hybridisation",q:`Arrange the following steps of somatic hybridisation in a correct sequence.
A. Digestion of cell walls.
B. Isolation of naked protoplasts.
C. Fusion of protoplasts to get hybrid protoplast.
D. Isolation of single cells from two different varieites of plants.
E. Growing of hybrid protoplast to form a new plant.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["E, A, B, C, D","D, A, B, C, E","E, B, A, D, C","D, B, A, E, C"],c:1,s:`Single cells (protoplasts) are isolated from two different varieties of plants, each with a desirable character.
Their cell walls are digested, giving naked protoplasts (surrounded by plasma membrane).
These fuse to give a hybrid protoplast, which is grown to form a new plant.
Such hybrids are somatic hybrids and the process is somatic hybridisation.
Correct sequence: D, A, B, C, E.`},{id:"neet-2026-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vector pBR322",q:"Insertion of a foreign DNA at BamHI site in an E.coli cloning vector pBR322 results in the loss of antibiotic resistance towards:",o:["Gentamycin","Ampicillin and tetracycline","Tetracycline","Ampicillin"],c:2,s:"If a foreign DNA is ligated at the BamHI site of the tetracycline resistance gene in pBR322, the recombinant plasmid loses tetracycline resistance because the gene is interrupted by the foreign DNA."},{id:"neet-2026r-q138",pn:138,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Recombinant DNA technology",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Transformation \u2014 I. Restriction enzyme
B. Cloning site \u2014 II. Transfer DNA to host bacteria
C. Selection \u2014 III. Replication
D. Ori \u2014 IV. Antibiotic
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],c:3,s:`Transformation is transfer of DNA to the host bacteria (A-II).
Cloning site is a DNA segment in a plasmid vector with unique recognition sequences for restriction enzymes (B-I).
Antibiotics help in selection of recombinants (C-IV).
Ori is the DNA sequence where the host's replication machinery starts copying the plasmid (D-III).`},{id:"neet-2026r-q143",pn:143,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR",q:"During PCR, primers bind to the DNA strands in the ______step.",o:["extension","annealing","ligation","denaturation"],c:1,s:`PCR has three steps: denaturation, annealing and extension.
Primers bind to the DNA strands in the annealing step.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q148",pn:148,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vectors, plasmids",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Plasmids are autonomously replicating DNA.
Statement II : Plasmids are extrachromosomal DNA.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`Plasmids are used as cloning vectors in genetic engineering.
Plasmids are autonomously replicating circular extra-chromosomal DNA.
They can replicate inside bacterial cells independent of the control of the chromosomal DNA.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q168",pn:168,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Isolation of genetic material",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: In recombinant DNA technology, lysozyme is used for disrupting bacterial cells while cellulase is for plant cells.
Reason R: Isolation of genetic material needs disruption of cells.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`DNA is enclosed within membranes, so the cell must be broken open to release it.
Other macromolecules such as RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids are released along with it.
This is done with enzymes: lysozyme (bacteria), cellulase (plant cells), chitinase (fungus).
So both A and R are correct and R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2025-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Polymerase chain reaction",q:"Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplifies DNA following the equation.",o:["N\xB2","2\u207F","2n + 1","2N\xB2"],c:1,s:`PCR amplifies DNA as per the equation 2\u207F, where n is the number of cycles.
Example: 3 PCR cycles give 2\xB3 = 2 \xD7 2 \xD7 2 = 8 DNA fragments.`},{id:"neet-2025-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Selection of recombinants",q:"In the above represented plasmid, an alien piece of DNA is inserted at EcoRI site. Which of the following strategies will be chosen to select the recombinant colonies?",o:["Using ampicillin & tetracycline containing medium plate.","Blue color colonies will be selected.","White color colonies will be selected.","Blue color colonies grown on ampicillin plates can be selected."],c:2,s:`White-coloured colonies will be selected.
The alien DNA is inserted at the EcoRI site, so the \u03B2-galactosidase gene there undergoes insertional inactivation.
This gene gives blue-coloured colonies, but since it is inactivated, white colonies are produced.
The ampicillin and tetracycline resistance genes stay intact, so the DNA will show ampR and tetR.`,fig:{w:318,h:190,alt:"A circular plasmid with Tet R, BamH1, Ori, amp, EcoRI and beta Galactosidase marked around it.",d:[["p","M 36.6 91.2 A 88.2 74.4 0 1 1 213 91.2 A 88.2 74.4 0 1 1 36.6 91.2 Z"],["p","M 109.2 10.8 L 165 13.2 L 192 39 L 184.8 49.2 L 156 40.8 L 112.2 24 Z","f"],["l",114,13.2,118.8,25.2],["l",129,18,133,29.8],["l",144,22.8,147.1,34.3],["l",159,27.6,161.3,38.9],["l",174,32.4,175.4,43.4],["l",189,37.2,189.6,48],["p","M 112.8 19.2 Q 162 21 190.8 43.2","B"],["p","M 25.2 57 L 73.8 67.2 L 73.8 99 L 19.8 118.8 Z","f"],["l",31.2,61.2,28.2,111.9],["l",37.8,62.5,34.8,109.6],["l",44.4,63.8,41.4,107.3],["l",51,65.2,48,105],["l",57.6,66.5,54.6,102.7],["l",64.2,67.8,61.2,100.4],["l",70.8,69.1,67.8,98],["p","M 87 145.8 L 145.2 150 L 145.2 159.6 L 93 162.6 Z","f"],["l",93,147.4,93,160.8],["l",98.4,147.8,98.4,160.8],["l",103.8,148.2,103.8,160.8],["l",109.2,148.6,109.2,160.8],["l",114.6,148.9,114.6,160.8],["l",120,149.3,120,160.8],["l",125.4,149.7,125.4,160.8],["l",130.8,150.1,130.8,160.8],["l",136.2,150.4,136.2,160.8],["l",141.6,150.8,141.6,160.8],["p","M 205.2 73.2 Q 213 114 175.2 145.2"],["p","M 211.2 73.2 Q 218.4 117 182.4 150"],["p","M 208.2 78 Q 216 114 177.6 147.6","B"],["l",219,73.8,232.2,82.8],["l",232.2,82.8,190.8,157.2],["l",181.2,150,190.8,157.2],["l",186,103.2,226.8,103.2],["l",214.8,124.8,231,130.8],["l",69,25.8,69,42],["t",25.2,28.8,"BamH1","s"],["t",169.8,14.4,"Tet R","s"],["t",1.2,91.2,"Ori","s"],["t",229.2,108,"EcoRI","s"],["t",225,141.6,"\u03B2 Galactosidase","s"],["t",100.8,180,"amp","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Bioreactors",q:"Identify the part of a bio-reactor which is used as a foam braker from the given figure.",o:["A","B","D","C"],c:3,s:`Part labelled C is the foam breaker.
B is the motor, A is the flat bladed impeller, D is sterile air inlet.`,fig:{w:210,h:266.5,alt:"Stirred-tank bioreactor with parts A, B, C and D pointed out by arrows.",d:[["p","M 23.4 61.1 V 217.1 Q 23.4 237.9 79.3 237.9 Q 136.5 237.9 136.5 217.1 V 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 61.1 Q 79.3 42.9 136.5 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 74.1 Q 79.3 89.7 136.5 74.1"],["l",79.3,22.1,79.3,235.3,""],["c",79.3,16.9,6.5],["l",13,68.9,23.4,68.9,""],["l",29.9,48.1,29.9,61.1,""],["l",54.6,87.1,104,87.1,""],["l",61.1,79.3,70.2,94.9,""],["l",88.4,79.3,97.5,94.9,""],["l",54.6,104,104,104,""],["l",61.1,96.2,70.2,111.8,""],["l",88.4,96.2,97.5,111.8,""],["l",54.6,149.5,104,149.5,""],["l",61.1,141.7,70.2,157.3,""],["l",88.4,141.7,97.5,157.3,""],["l",54.6,191.1,104,191.1,""],["l",61.1,183.3,70.2,198.9,""],["l",88.4,183.3,97.5,198.9,""],["l",79.3,235.3,79.3,248.3,""],["l",65,248.3,93.6,248.3,""],["l",93.6,237.9,117,237.9,""],["l",117,237.9,117,245.7,""],["l",94.9,41.6,163.8,41.6,"a"],["t",171.6,46.8,"B",""],["l",94.9,70.2,163.8,70.2,"a"],["t",171.6,75.4,"C",""],["l",94.9,101.4,163.8,101.4,"a"],["t",171.6,106.6,"A",""],["l",94.9,243.1,163.8,243.1,"a"],["t",171.6,248.3,"D",""]]},sfig:{w:330,h:266.5,alt:"Bioreactor with B motor, C foam breaker, A flat bladed impeller and D sterile air inlet marked.",d:[["p","M 23.4 61.1 V 217.1 Q 23.4 237.9 79.3 237.9 Q 136.5 237.9 136.5 217.1 V 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 61.1 Q 79.3 42.9 136.5 61.1"],["p","M 23.4 74.1 Q 79.3 89.7 136.5 74.1"],["l",79.3,22.1,79.3,235.3,""],["c",79.3,16.9,6.5],["l",13,68.9,23.4,68.9,""],["l",29.9,48.1,29.9,61.1,""],["l",54.6,87.1,104,87.1,""],["l",61.1,79.3,70.2,94.9,""],["l",88.4,79.3,97.5,94.9,""],["l",54.6,104,104,104,""],["l",61.1,96.2,70.2,111.8,""],["l",88.4,96.2,97.5,111.8,""],["l",54.6,149.5,104,149.5,""],["l",61.1,141.7,70.2,157.3,""],["l",88.4,141.7,97.5,157.3,""],["l",54.6,191.1,104,191.1,""],["l",61.1,183.3,70.2,198.9,""],["l",88.4,183.3,97.5,198.9,""],["l",79.3,235.3,79.3,248.3,""],["l",65,248.3,93.6,248.3,""],["l",93.6,237.9,117,237.9,""],["l",117,237.9,117,245.7,""],["l",94.9,41.6,163.8,41.6,"a"],["t",171.6,46.8,"B - Motor","s"],["l",94.9,70.2,163.8,70.2,"a"],["t",171.6,75.4,"C - Foam breaker","s"],["l",94.9,101.4,163.8,101.4,"a"],["t",171.6,106.6,"A - Flat bladded impeller","s"],["l",94.9,243.1,163.8,243.1,"a"],["t",171.6,248.3,"D - Sterile air","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : The DNA fragments extracted from gel electrophoresis can be used in construction of recombinant DNA.
Statement II : Smaller size DNA fragments are observed near anode while larger fragments are found near the wells in an agarose gel.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],c:0,s:`Cutting DNA with restriction endonucleases gives DNA fragments, which are separated by gel electrophoresis.
Separated DNA bands are cut out of the agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece. This step is elution.
The purified fragments are used to build rDNA by joining them with cloning vectors.
In gel electrophoresis, fragments separate by size through the sieving effect of agarose.
The smaller the fragment, the farther it moves from cathode towards anode.`},{id:"neet-2025-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gene cloning enzymes",q:`Which of the following enzyme(s) are NOT essential for gene cloning?
A. Restriction enzymes
B. DNA ligase
C. DNA mutase
D. DNA recombinase
E. DNA polymerase
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["C and D only","A and B only","D and E only","B and C only"],c:0,s:`Gene cloning is a process where a specific gene or DNA sequence is isolated and copied, making many identical copies.
In gene cloning, restriction enzymes, DNA ligase and DNA polymerase are mainly used.`},{id:"neet-2025-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Blue-white selection",q:`The blue and white selectable markers have been developed which differentiate recombinant colonies from non-recombinant colonies on the basis of their ability to produce colour in the presence of a chromogenic substrate.
Given below are two statements about this method:
Statement I : The blue coloured colonies have DNA insert in the plasmid and they are identified as recombinant colonies.
Statement II : The colonies without blue colour have DNA insert in the plasmid and are identified as recombinant colonies.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],c:3,s:`Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.
Recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of the enzyme \u03B2-galactosidase.
This inactivates the gene for making this enzyme (insertional inactivation).
So colonies with an insert do not produce colour and are identified as recombinant.
Non-recombinant colonies make blue colour in the presence of the chromogenic substrate.`},{id:"neet-2024-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"Hind II always cuts DNA molecules at a particular point called recognition sequence and it consists of :",o:["4 bp","10 bp","8 bp","6 bp"],c:3,s:`Hind II was the first restriction endonuclease whose working was found to depend on a specific DNA sequence.
It always cuts DNA at a particular point by recognising a sequence of six base pairs.
The other options have more or fewer than 6 bp, so they are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2024-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning and gene transfer",q:`What is the fate of piece of DNA carrying only gene of interest which is transferred into an alien organism?
A. The piece of DNA would be able to multiply itself independently in the progeny cells of the organisms.
B. It may get generated into the genome of the recipient.
C. It may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA.
D. The alien piece of DNA is not an integral part of chromosome.
E. It shows ability to replicate.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and C only","A and E only","A and B only","D and E only"],c:0,s:`A piece of DNA carrying only the gene of interest cannot multiply by itself in the progeny cells.
But if it gets integrated into the genome of the recipient, it may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA.
So B and C are the correct statements.`},{id:"neet-2024-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Somatic hybridisation",q:"Which of the following are fused in somatic hybridization involving two varieties of plants?",o:["Protoplasts","Pollens","Callus","Somatic embryos"],c:0,s:"In somatic hybridisation, the protoplasts of two plant varieties are fused."},{id:"neet-2024-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vector pBR322",q:"The following diagram showing restriction sites in E.coli cloning vector pBR322. Find the role of 'X' and 'Y' genes :",o:["The gene 'X' is for protein involved in replication of Plasmid and 'Y' for resistance to antibiotics.","Gene 'X' is responsible for recognition sites and 'Y' is responsible for antibiotic resistance.","The gene 'X' is responsible for resistance to antibiotics and 'Y' for protein involved in the replication of Plasmid.","The gene 'X' is responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA and 'Y' for protein involved in the replication of Plasmid."],c:3,s:`Option (4) is correct.
X is ori, which controls the copy number of the linked DNA.
Y is rop, which codes for a protein involved in plasmid replication.
Options (1), (2) and (3) link X and Y to wrong functions.`,fig:{w:230,h:180,alt:"Circular map of plasmid pBR322 with restriction sites EcoR I, Cla I, Hind III, Pvu I, Pst, BamH I, Sal I and Pvu II marked outside, and genes X and Y marked on lower-left of the ring.",d:[["c",110,92,55],["c",110,92,43],["p","M93.0 39.7 A55 55 0 0 1 132.4 41.8 L127.5 52.7 A43 43 0 0 0 96.7 51.1Z","F"],["p","M132.4 41.8 A55 55 0 0 1 137.5 139.6 L131.5 129.2 A43 43 0 0 0 127.5 52.7Z","f"],["p","M157.6 119.5 A55 55 0 0 1 131.0 142.8 L126.5 131.7 A43 43 0 0 0 147.2 113.5 Z","F"],["p","M131.0 142.8 A55 55 0 0 1 102.8 146.5 L104.4 134.6 A43 43 0 0 0 126.5 131.7 Z",""],["p","M102.8 146.5 A55 55 0 0 1 82.5 139.6 L88.5 129.2 A43 43 0 0 0 104.4 134.6 Z","f"],["p","M82.5 139.6 A55 55 0 0 1 66.4 125.5 L75.9 118.2 A43 43 0 0 0 88.5 129.2 Z","f"],["p","M66.4 125.5 A55 55 0 0 1 56.9 106.2 L68.5 103.1 A43 43 0 0 0 75.9 118.2 Z","F"],["p","M56.9 106.2 A55 55 0 0 1 93.9 39.4 L97.4 50.9 A43 43 0 0 0 68.5 103.1 Z",""],["t",110,78,"pBR322","ms"],["l",38,22,98,40],["t",34,26,"EcoR I","se"],["t",58,12,"Cla I","s"],["l",95,16,95,38],["t",110,14,"Hind III","s"],["l",104,22,104,38],["t",34,50,"Pvu I","se"],["l",38,46,76,60],["t",28,68,"Pst","se"],["l",32,64,70,76],["t",166,48,"BamH I","s"],["l",164,45,142,52],["t",182,92,"Sal I","s"],["l",178,90,163,92],["t",106,170,"Pvu II","s"],["l",110,162,110,148],["t",90,108,"X","m"],["l",86,112,80,121],["t",117,114,"Y","ms"],["l",113,118,113,133]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Bioreactors",q:"Which of the following statements is incorrect?",o:["Bio-reactors are used to produce small scale bacterial cultures.","Bio-reactors have an agitator system, an oxygen delivery system and foam control system.","A bio-reactor provides optimal growth conditions for achieving the desired product.","Most commonly used bio-reactors are of stirring type."],c:0,s:`Statement (1) is incorrect.
Bioreactors process large volumes (100 to 1000 litres) of culture.
Small cultures cannot give appreciable amounts of product, so bioreactors are developed for large-scale production.`},{id:"neet-2023-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis and staining",q:"Upon exposure to UV radiation, DNA stained with ethidium bromide will show",o:["Bright orange colour","Bright red colour","Bright blue colour","Bright yellow colour"],c:0,s:`Separated DNA fragments are seen only after staining with ethidium bromide and exposing to UV light.
The DNA bands then glow bright orange in the gel.`},{id:"neet-2023-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Isolation of DNA",q:"During the purification process for recombinant DNA technology, addition of chilled ethanol precipitates out",o:["Polysaccharides","RNA","DNA","Histones"],c:2,s:`While isolating genetic material, purified DNA comes out as threads when chilled ethanol is added.
Proteins such as histones are removed earlier with protease.
RNA is removed with ribonuclease.`},{id:"neet-2023-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gene gun method",q:"In gene gun method used to introduce alien DNA into host cells, microparticles of ________ metal are used.",o:["Silver","Copper","Zinc","Tungsten or gold"],c:3,s:`The gene gun uses microparticles of tungsten or gold.
These metals are inert, so they do not change the chemical makeup of the cells.`},{id:"neet-2023-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Steps of recombinant DNA",q:`Main steps in the formation of Recombinant DNA are given below. Arrange these steps in a correct sequence.
A. Insertion of recombinant DNA into the host cell
B. Cutting of DNA at specific location by restriction enzyme
C. Isolation of desired DNA fragment
D. Amplification of gene of interest using PCR
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["B, D, A, C","B, C, D, A","C, A, B, D","C, B, D, A"],c:1,s:`First DNA is cut by a restriction enzyme (B).
Then the desired fragment is isolated (C).
The gene of interest is amplified by PCR (D).
Finally the recombinant DNA is put into the host cell (A).
Order: B, C, D, A.`},{id:"neet-2023-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vectors",q:"Which of the following is not a cloning vector?",o:["Probe","BAC","YAC","pBR322"],c:0,s:`A probe is a single-stranded DNA or RNA tagged with a radioactive molecule. It is used to detect a mutated gene, not to clone.
BAC, YAC and pBR322 are all cloning vectors.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q103",pn:103,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes sticky ends",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : When a particular restriction enzyme cuts strand of DNA, overhanging stretches or sticky ends are formed.
Reason (R): Some restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindromic site.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct"],c:1,s:`Both statements are correct.
Sticky ends form when an enzyme cuts a little away from the centre of the palindromic site.
The cut falls between the same two bases on the opposite strand, leaving overhangs.
So R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2022-q106",pn:106,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR and selectable markers",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Polymerase chain reaction is used in DNA amplification.
Reason (R) : The ampicillin resistant gene is used as a selectable marker to check transformation
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],c:0,s:`Both statements are true.
PCR amplifies DNA.
The ampicillin resistance gene helps select transformants.
But the reason does not explain the assertion.`},{id:"neet-2022-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"Which one of the following statement is not true regarding gel electrophoresis technique?",o:["The separated DNA fragments are stained by using ethidium bromide.","The presence of chromogenic substrate gives blue coloured DNA bands on the gel.","Bright orange coloured bands of DNA can be observed in the gel when exposed to UV light.","The process of extraction of separated DNA strands from gel is called elution."],c:1,s:`Statement 2 is wrong.
DNA bands stained with ethidium bromide glow bright orange under UV light.
A chromogenic substrate is not what colours the bands.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q133",pn:133,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"Separation of DNA fragments is done by a technique known as",o:["Gel electrophoresis","Polymerase Chain Reaction","Recombinant technology","Southern blotting"],c:0,s:`Restriction enzymes cut DNA into fragments.
These fragments are separated by gel electrophoresis.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q144",pn:144,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Enzymes for isolating DNA",q:"The enzyme (a) is needed for isolating genetic material from plant cells and enzyme (b) for isolating genetic material from fungus. Choose the correct pair of options from the following:",o:["(a) Cellulase (b) Lipase","(a) Cellulase (b) Protease","(a) Cellulase (b) Chitinase","(a) Chitinase (b) Lipase"],c:2,s:`To get the DNA out, the cell wall must be broken first.
Plant cell walls are digested by cellulase.
Fungal cell walls are digested by chitinase.`},{id:"neet-2022-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Palindromic sequences and restriction enzymes",q:"In the following palindromic base sequences of DNA, which one can be cut easily by particular restriction enzyme?",o:["5\u2032GAATTC3\u2032; 3\u2032CTTAAG5\u2032","5\u2032CTCAGT3\u2032; 3\u2032GAGTCA5\u2032","5\u2032GTATTC3\u2032; 3\u2032CATAAG5\u2032","5\u2032GATACT3\u2032; 3\u2032CTATGA5\u2032"],c:0,s:`A palindromic sequence reads the same on both strands when both are read in the same 5\u2032\u21923\u2032 direction.
Only option (1) is palindromic: 5\u2032GAATTC3\u2032 and 3\u2032CTTAAG5\u2032 both read GAATTC.
So only it is cut by a restriction enzyme (EcoRI).`},{id:"neet-2022r-q164",pn:164,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Plasmids as vectors",q:"Pathogenic bacteria gain resistance to antibiotics due to changes in their :",o:["Nucleoid","Cosmids","Plasmids","Nucleus"],c:2,s:"R-plasmids (resistance plasmids) give bacteria resistance to antibiotics."},{id:"neet-2022-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction endonucleases",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Restriction endonucleases recognise specific sequence to cut DNA known as palindromic nucleotide sequence.
Statement II: Restriction endonucleases cut the DNA strand a little away from the centre of the palindromic site.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic sequence, binds the DNA and cuts both strands at specific points.
It cuts a little away from the centre of the palindrome, between the same two bases on the opposite strands.
So both statements are correct.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q183",pn:183,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Vectors and gene transfer",q:"Which of the following methods is not commonly used for introducing foreign DNA into the plant cell?",o:["Bacteriophages","Agrobacterium mediated transformation","Gene gun","'Disarmed pathogen' vectors"],c:0,s:`Bacteriophages carry foreign DNA into bacteria, not into plants.
Agrobacterium, gene gun and disarmed pathogen vectors are used for plant cells.`},{id:"neet-2022-q197",pn:197,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vectors",q:"Which of the following is not a desirable feature of a cloning vector?",o:["Presence of a marker gene","Presence of single restriction enzyme site","Presence of two or more recognition site","Presence of origin of replication"],c:2,s:`A cloning vector carries the desired gene into the host.
Desirable features: an origin of replication, a marker gene, and very few, preferably a single, recognition site for common restriction enzymes.
Two or more recognition sites is not desirable.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q198",pn:198,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:`Refer to the following statements for agarose gel electrophoresis :
(a) Agarose is a natural polymer obtained from sea-weed.
(b) The separation of DNA molecules in agarose-gel electrophoresis depends on the size of DNA.
(c) The DNA migrates from negatively-charged electrode to the positively-charged electrode
(d) The DNA migrates from positively-charged electrode to the negatively-charged electrode.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below`,o:["(b), (c) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only","(a), (b) and (c) only","(a), (b) and (d) only"],c:2,s:`DNA fragments are negatively charged.
Under an electric field they move toward the positive electrode (anode), so (c) is true and (d) is false.
Agarose comes from sea-weed and separation depends on fragment size, so (a) and (b) are true.`},{id:"neet-2021-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"DNA strands on a gel stained with ethidium bromide when viewed under UV radiation, appear as",o:["Bright blue bands","Yellow bands","Bright orange bands","Dark red bands"],c:2,s:`Ethidium bromide slips between the DNA bases and fluoresces.
Under UV light the stained DNA bands glow bright orange.`},{id:"neet-2021-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Plant tissue culture",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Protoplast fusion (b) Plant tissue culture (c) Meristem culture (d) Micropropagation
List-II: (i) Totipotency (ii) Pomato (iii) Somaclones (iv) Virus free plants`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],c:2,s:`Protoplast fusion of potato and tomato gives pomato: a-ii.
Plant tissue culture works because cells are totipotent: b-i.
Meristem tips are virus free, so meristem culture gives virus-free plants: c-iv.
Micropropagation produces genetically identical plants, somaclones: d-iii.`},{id:"neet-2021-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR",q:"Which of the following is a correct sequence of steps in a PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)?",o:["Annealing, Denaturation, Extension","Denaturation, Annealing, Extension","Denaturation, Extension, Annealing","Extension, Denaturation, Annealing"],c:1,s:`Step 1, denaturation: heating (about 94 \xB0C) separates the DNA strands.
Step 2, annealing: primers bind to the single strands.
Step 3, extension: Taq polymerase adds nucleotides to the primers.
Order: denaturation, annealing, extension.`},{id:"neet-2021-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Applications of PCR",q:"Which of the following is not an application of PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction) ?",o:["Detection of gene mutation","Molecular diagnosis","Gene amplification","Purification of isolated protein"],c:3,s:`PCR makes many copies of a DNA segment.
It is used to amplify genes, detect mutations and detect infectious agents (molecular diagnosis).
It works on DNA, not protein, so it is not used to purify proteins.`},{id:"neet-2021-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Insertional inactivation",q:"Plasmid pBR322 has PstI restriction enzyme site within gene ampR that confers ampicillin resistance. If this enzyme is used for inserting a gene for \u03B2-galactoside production and the recombinant plasmid is inserted in an E.coli strain",o:["It will be able to produce a novel protein with dual ability","It will not be able to confer ampicillin resistance to the host cell","The transformed cells will have the ability to resist ampicillin as well as produce \u03B2-galactoside","It will lead to lysis of host cell"],c:1,s:`PstI cuts inside the ampR gene.
The new gene is inserted into that gene, so ampR is broken (insertional inactivation).
The host therefore loses ampicillin resistance; it makes \u03B2-galactosidase, but this is neither a novel dual protein nor does it cause lysis.`},{id:"neet-2021-q157",pn:157,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR steps",q:"During the process of gene amplification using PCR, if very high temperature is not maintained in the beginning, then which of the following steps of PCR will be affected first?",o:["Ligation","Annealing","Extension","Denaturation"],c:3,s:`The first step of PCR is denaturation at about 94 \xB0C, which separates the DNA strands.
Without the high temperature this step fails first.
Annealing (50-60 \xB0C) comes next, and ligation is not a PCR step.`},{id:"neet-2021-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"A specific recognition sequence identified by endonucleases to make cuts at specific positions within the DNA is:",o:["Poly(A) tail sequences","Degenerate primer sequence","Okazaki sequences","Palindromic Nucleotide sequences"],c:3,s:`A restriction endonuclease recognises a palindromic sequence (reads the same on both strands in the 5' to 3' direction) and cuts both strands there.
Poly(A) tails, degenerate primers and Okazaki fragments are unrelated.`},{id:"neet-2020-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"In gel electrophoresis, separated DNA fragments can be visualized with the help of :",o:["Ethidium bromide in UV radiation","Acetocarmine in UV radiation","Ethidium bromide in infrared radiation","Acetocarmine in bright blue light"],c:0,s:`DNA fragments in the gel are stained with ethidium bromide.
Under UV light the stained DNA shows as orange bands.`},{id:"neet-2020-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vector",q:"The sequence that controls the copy number of the linked DNA in the vector, is termed :",o:["Ori site","Palindromic sequence","Recognition site","Selectable marker"],c:0,s:"The origin of replication (ori) starts replication and controls the copy number of the linked DNA."},{id:"neet-2020-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"The specific palindromic sequence which is recognized by EcoRI is :",o:["5'-GGAACC-3', 3'-CCTTGG-5'","5'-CTTAAG-3', 3'-GAATTC-5'","5'-GGATCC-3', 3-CCTAGG-5'","5'-GAATTC-3', 3'-CTTAAG-5'"],c:3,s:"EcoRI recognises the palindromic sequence 5'-GAATTC-3' with 3'-CTTAAG-5'."},{id:"neet-2020-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"Identify the wrong statement with regard to Restriction Enzymes.",o:["They cut the strand of DNA at palindromic sites.","They are useful in genetic engineering.","Sticky ends can be joined by using DNA ligases.","Each restriction enzyme functions by inspection the of a DNA sequence."],c:0,s:`Restriction enzymes inspect the DNA and cut at a specific recognition sequence, which is palindromic.
Sticky ends made by them can be joined by DNA ligase.
The printed key (1) is kept.
Note: Printed answer 1 kept. Statement (4) is garbled in the printed paper, and statement (1) is true for restriction enzymes (they cut at palindromic sites), so the printed key may be wrong.`},{id:"neet-2020-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Tools of biotechnology",q:`Match the organism with its use in biotechnology
(a) Bacillus thuringiensis  (i) Cloning vector
(b) Thermus aquaticus  (ii) Construction of first DNA molecule
(c) Agrobacterium tumefaciens  (iii) DNA polymerase
(d) Salmonella typhimurium  (iv) Cry proteins
Select the correct option from the following :`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:0,s:`Bacillus thuringiensis gives Cry proteins.
Thermus aquaticus gives Taq DNA polymerase.
Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a cloning vector.
Salmonella typhimurium was used in the construction of the first recombinant DNA molecule.`},{id:"neet-2020-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Enzymes for DNA work",q:"Choose the correct pair from the following :",o:["Polymerases \u2013 Break the DNA into fragments","Nucleases \u2013 Separate the two strands of DNA","Exonucleases \u2013 Make cuts at specific positions within DNA","Ligases \u2013 Join the two DNA molecules"],c:3,s:`Ligases join two DNA molecules.
Nucleases cut DNA, exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends, and polymerases make DNA.`},{id:"neet-2019-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Isolation of DNA",q:"DNA precipitation out of a mixture of bimolecules can be achieved by treatment with :",o:["Methanol at room temperature","Chilled chloroform","Isopropanol","Chilled ethanol"],c:3,s:`DNA is soluble in water but not in cold ethanol.
Adding chilled ethanol makes DNA come out as fine threads.
These threads can be spooled out.`},{id:"neet-2019-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"Following statements describe the characteristics of the enzyme Restriction Endonuclease. Identify the incorrect statement.",o:["The enzyme cuts the sugar-phosphate backbone at specific sites on each strand","The enzyme recognizes a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence in the DNA","The enzyme cuts DNA molecule at identified position within the DNA","The enzyme binds DNA at specific sites and cuts only one of the two strands"],c:3,s:`A restriction endonuclease recognises a palindromic site.
It cuts the sugar-phosphate backbone of both strands, a little apart.
So the claim that it cuts only one strand is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2018-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Vectors",q:"Which of the following is commonly used as a vector for introducing a DNA fragment in human lymphocytes ?",o:["Retrovirus","Ti plasmid","\u03BB phage","pBR 322"],c:0,s:`Retroviruses can enter animal cells and integrate their genes into the host genome.
Disarmed retroviruses are used to carry genes into human lymphocytes (for example in gene therapy for ADA deficiency).
Ti plasmid is for plants, and \u03BB phage and pBR322 are used in bacteria.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2018-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Steps of PCR",q:"The correct order of steps in Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is -",o:["Extension, Denaturation, Annealing","Annealing, Extension, Denaturation","Denaturation, Extension, Annealing","Denaturation, Annealing, Extension"],c:3,s:`Denaturation: heat separates the two strands of the DNA.
Annealing: primers bind to the single strands.
Extension: Taq polymerase adds nucleotides to make new strands.
So the order is denaturation, annealing, extension.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2017-q050",pn:50,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Selectable marker",q:"A gene whose expression helps to identify transformed cell is known as",o:["Structural gene","Selectable marker","Vector","Plasmid"],c:1,s:`A selectable marker gene (for example an antibiotic resistance gene) is carried on the vector.
Only transformed cells express it, so they survive on the selective medium and can be identified.
A vector and a plasmid are carriers, not the identifying gene.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2017-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"DNA fragments are",o:["Either positively or negatively charged depending on their size","Positively charged","Negatively charged","Neutral"],c:2,s:`Each nucleotide has a phosphate group in the DNA backbone.
Phosphate carries a negative charge, so DNA fragments are negatively charged.
In gel electrophoresis they move toward the positive electrode.`},{id:"neet-2017-q090",pn:90,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Downstream processing",q:"The process of separation and purification of expressed protein before marketing is called :",o:["Postproduction processing","Upstream processing","Downstream processing","Bioprocessing"],c:2,s:`After the product is made in the bioreactor, it is separated and purified, and tested for quality.
These steps together are downstream processing.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2017-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"The DNA fragments separated on an agarose gel can be visualised after staining with :",o:["Ethidium bromide","Bromophenol blue","Acetocarmine","Aniline blue"],c:0,s:`Ethidium bromide binds DNA and glows orange under UV light.
So the DNA bands become visible.
Bromophenol blue is only a tracking dye in the loading buffer.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2017-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"What is the criterion for DNA fragments movement on agarose gel during gel electrophoresis ?",o:["Negatively charged fragments do not move","The larger the fragment size, the farther it moves","The smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves","Positively charged fragment move to farther end"],c:2,s:`DNA is negatively charged and moves toward the positive electrode.
The agarose gel acts like a sieve.
Small fragments pass through easily and move farther than large ones.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`}]})();
